.Dd November 6, 2026
.Dt FILEOPS 9
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm fileops ,
.Nm fo_pathconf ,
.Nm fo_statfs ,
.Nm fo_fsync
.Nd operations on an open file descriptor
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/param.h
.In sys/file.h
.Ft int
.Fn fo_pathconf "struct file *fp" "int name" "long *valuep" "struct thread *td"
.Ft int
.Fn fo_statfs "struct file *fp" "struct statfs *buf" "struct thread *td"
.Ft int
.Fn fo_fsync "struct file *fp" "int mode" "struct thread *td"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Every open file
.Pq Vt "struct file"
carries a pointer,
.Va f_ops ,
to a
.Vt "struct fileops"
table that implements each operation a process can perform on the
descriptor.
System calls that take a file descriptor dispatch through this table and
never through the descriptor's type
.Pq Va f_type
or through a vnode the file may carry
.Pq Va f_vnode .
A descriptor type therefore answers for itself, and a type that is not
backed by a vnode is complete without one.
.Pp
Each member has an inline dispatch function of the same name.
Members that a table leaves
.Dv NULL
dispatch to a type-neutral default, named
.Fn invfo_* ,
which returns the error the operation has always returned for a descriptor
of no particular type.
Vnode-backed descriptors use the
.Fn vn_*
implementations.
.Ss Descriptors that carry a vnode without being vnode files
Several descriptor types keep a reference to the vnode they were opened
through although they are not
.Dv DTYPE_VNODE
files served by
.Va vnops :
FIFOs
.Pq Va pipeops ,
pseudo-terminal masters,
.Xr devfs 4
nodes under their own tables, and linuxkpi files.
For every operation that is answered by the vnode, these tables install the
.Fn vn_*
implementation explicitly, so the descriptor answers exactly as its vnode
does; no default inspects
.Va f_vnode .
A table that does not carry a vnode must not install a
.Fn vn_*
member.
.Ss fo_pathconf
.Fn fo_pathconf
answers
.Xr fpathconf 2 :
it stores the value of the configuration variable
.Fa name
through
.Fa valuep
and returns 0, or returns an error.
.Dv _PC_ASYNC_IO
is answered by the system call for every descriptor and never reaches the
table.
.Bl -tag -width "devfs nodes"
.It vnode files
.Fn vn_pathconf
locks the vnode shared and calls
.Xr VOP_PATHCONF 9 .
Directories, regular files,
.Dv O_PATH
files, FIFOs, pseudo-terminal masters, devfs nodes and linuxkpi files all
use it.
.It pipes and sockets
Answer
.Dv _PC_PIPE_BUF
with
.Dv PIPE_BUF
and return
.Er EINVAL
for every other name.
A FIFO is a pipe that carries a vnode and answers through the vnode
instead.
.It everything else
.Fn invfo_pathconf
returns
.Er EOPNOTSUPP .
.El
.Ss fo_statfs
.Fn fo_statfs
answers
.Xr fstatfs 2
by filling
.Fa buf .
.Bl -tag -width "devfs nodes"
.It vnode files
.Fn vn_statfs
references the vnode's mount, runs the MAC mount-stat check, and calls
.Fn kern_do_statfs ,
which busies the mount, calls
.Xr VFS_STATFS 9
and applies jail
.Xr statfs 2
enforcement.
All of it needs the mount, so none of it is left in the system call.
Directories, regular files,
.Dv O_PATH
files, FIFOs, pseudo-terminal masters, devfs nodes and linuxkpi files all
use it.
.It everything else
.Fn invfo_statfs
returns
.Er EINVAL ,
the error a descriptor without a vnode has always received.
.El
.Ss fo_fsync
.Fn fo_fsync
flushes the file according to
.Fa mode :
.Bl -tag -width "FO_FSYNC_FULL" -compact
.It Dv FO_FSYNC_FULL
data and metadata, as
.Xr fsync 2
and
.Dv LIO_SYNC ;
.It Dv FO_FSYNC_DATA
data only, as
.Xr fdatasync 2
and
.Dv LIO_DSYNC ;
.It Dv FO_FSYNC_FS
the whole filesystem the file is on, as Linux
.Fn syncfs .
.El
.Bl -tag -width "devfs nodes"
.It vnode files
.Fn vn_fsync
is the one implementation for the system calls,
.Xr aio_fsync 2
and
.Fn syncfs .
It suspends writes on the mount, locks the vnode, cleans its pages and
calls
.Xr VOP_FSYNC 9
or
.Xr VOP_FDATASYNC 9 ,
retrying on
.Er ERELOOKUP ;
for
.Dv FO_FSYNC_FS
it busies the mount and runs
.Xr VFS_SYNC 9 .
It enters the file context itself, so every table installs it directly.
Directories, regular files, FIFOs, pseudo-terminal masters, devfs nodes,
linuxkpi files and
.Dv O_PATH
files use it;
.Xr fsync 2
and
.Xr fdatasync 2
refuse an
.Dv O_PATH
descriptor with
.Er EBADF
before reaching the table, as they always did, while
.Xr aio_fsync 2
and
.Fn syncfs
accept one, as they always did.
.It everything else
.Fn invfo_fsync
returns
.Er EINVAL .
.El
.Sh RETURN VALUES
Each operation returns 0 on success or an
.Xr errno 2
value.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr aio_fsync 2 ,
.Xr fdatasync 2 ,
.Xr fpathconf 2 ,
.Xr fstatfs 2 ,
.Xr fsync 2 ,
.Xr VFS_STATFS 9 ,
.Xr VFS_SYNC 9 ,
.Xr vnode 9 ,
.Xr VOP_FSYNC 9 ,
.Xr VOP_PATHCONF 9
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Vt "struct fileops"
table has existed since
.Bx 4.4 .
.Fn fo_pathconf ,
.Fn fo_statfs
and
.Fn fo_fsync
first appeared in 5BSD, in the series that made every descriptor type
answer its own system calls without the kernel reaching past the table to
a vnode.
